<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mzilikazi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1790–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mzilikazi was a nineteenth-century military leader who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> kingdom in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. His Ndebele moved onto the plateau from the south around 1840, completing the occupation of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi</a> heartland. The Ndebele state drew in part on earlier political precedents, and its arrival marked the end of the Rozvi Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mzilikazi.jpg" alt="Mzilikazi" width="275" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mzilikazi~detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rozvi political precedents also informed successor arrangements on the plateau, including the Ndebele kingdom established by <b>Mzilikazi</b> from around 1840.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s rise reshaped southern Africa: groups fleeing Zulu expansion or breaking away from it founded new states, among them the Ndebele Kingdom of <b>Mzilikazi</b>, while the Gaza kingdom of Soshangane emerged from the defeated Ndwandwe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mzilikazi</b>, a general of Shaka’s, fled his service and founded a kingdom of his own.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mzilikazi</b>, a Khumalo commander under Shaka, broke away around 1822 with a few hundred followers and moved onto the highveld, absorbing Sotho-Tswana communities as he went.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The king ruled through royal kin and regimental age-sets, and the kingdom recovered from defeat by <b>Mzilikazi</b> in the 1820s under Mabhoko (Mapoch), who used the caves of KwaMaza as a defensive refuge.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndzundza-ndebele-kingdom">Ndzundza Ndebele Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
